  "query_preview": "..\n\nConfluent flow amidst a rising mid-level height field prolongs a \nperiod of deep subsidence over the region into this evening. Mostly \nclear skies today have been augmented by shallow lake effect clouds \nwithin breezy northwest cold advection. As the anticylone works in \ntonight and early Tuesday, winds go nearly calm and the cold \nadvection ceases. Max temps only in the lower 60s this afternoon and \ndew points mixing into the 30s will prime temps to quickly fall into \nthe 40s this evening before settling into the 30s overnight for most \nareas away from the Metro Detroit heat island and immediate \nlakeshores. Given the expectation for decoupling of the boundary \nlayer, deep layer dry air, and mostly clear skies, frost formation \nwill be likely for much of northern SE MI early Tuesday morning. \nHighest confidence in this will be along and north of the I-69 \ncorridor where the surface ridge tracks and NBM hourly probabilities \nfor temps below 37F have jumped to the 70 to 90% range. Thin high \ncloud still likely to make an appearance before dawn, but shouldn't \ndampen frost prospects much. A Frost Advisory has been issued in \ncollaboration with neighboring offices.\n\nNotable warm advection commences daytime Tuesday as the ridge axis \npasses east and milder air is drawn northward by the next wave \npassing into northern Ontario. System relative isentropic ascent \nspreads overhead in waves through the day, but the best dynamics and \nmoisture advection are displaced well north of the local area. \nEnsemble space shows potential for a few sprinkles as far south as \nthe Saginaw Bay region, otherwise just some passing mid and high \nclouds at times as milder air brings highs into the mid to upper 60s.\n\nThe system's cold front is modeled to sink across the area Wednesday \nafternoon, through an environment that continues to lack deeper \nmoisture and upper support for ascent. A wedge of higher 1000-850mb \ntheta-e precedes the front, allowing temps to peak in the lower 70s \nbefore they fall into the 40s behind the front Wednesday night. \nSlightly cooler, but seasonable temperatures dominate the late week \nas a broad thermal trough settles over much of the Great Lakes. Some \nlake effect showers possible in this pattern, but the bulk are \nforecast to hold across the northern lakes. A stronger ridge is \nfavored to develop over the region this weekend, with a broad \nMidwest LLJ advecting much warmer temperatures into the vicinity by \nSunday. Solution spread remains high in the ensemble space, but \nfairly high confidence exists for highs in the 70s and a handful of \nensemble members suggest potential for temperatures in the lower 80s.",
